{
  "slug": "spuds",
  "title": "Spuds: a finite hot-potato pot game",
  "kind": "coin_puzzle",
  "security_scope": "Original WIP teaching policy for issue #10, not ready for real funds. One self-recreating pot coin curries one proper list (its own module hash, the current holder's 48-byte BLS key, the holder's payout puzzle hash, a finite round 1..8). Round r holds exactly r*1000 mojos. A take, signed by the new holder over the successor puzzle hash and allowed only before the coin is five blocks old, recreates the same module curried with the new holder and round r+1 holding exactly 1000 more mojos supplied from outside the pot. A claim, signed by the current holder and allowed only once the coin is at least five blocks old, pays the whole pot to the committed payout hash. Every path asserts its exact amount, a minimum one-block age and announces its intent message. Consensus enforces signatures, ages and amounts; the puzzle only emits the conditions. Round 8 can only be claimed.",
  "environment": "strict",
  "reference_mode": "module",
  "provenance": {
    "classification": "original teaching implementation",
    "references": [
      "https://chialisp.com/conditions/",
      "https://chialisp.com/chialisp-currying/",
      "https://github.com/judeallred/chialisp-oxide/issues/10"
    ],
    "source_origin": "The Rust export and Chialisp reference are promoted unchanged (apart from the export name) from the original experiments/spuds policy; the Rue port is a new independent translation. No upstream game puzzle is copied or ported.",
    "limitations": [
      "A take is exactly 1000 mojos, not an open bid; there is no refund or share for the previous holder.",
      "Takes race: anyone holding a key can sign a take for themselves, and block producers can order or censor competing takes (MEV-exposed). A take does not guarantee the pot will not be taken again.",
      "The 1000-mojo top-up comes from another coin in the same bundle; the puzzle does not choose or authenticate that source.",
      "Fixture keys are public test data. SHA-256 and BLS security are assumptions; proof models do not cover SHA, BLS, this export or the multi-round game."
    ]
  },
